hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2852","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Za'idah, who said: My father narrated to me, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said:\n\nAbdullah ibn Mas'ud (RA) set out from 'Arafat in his usual manner, without urging his camel, until he reached Muzdalifah. There, he dismounted and gave the call to prayer (Adhan), then the call to establish the prayer (Iqamah), and prayed Maghrib. Then he had his supper, after which he stood up, gave the Adhan, the Iqamah, and prayed 'Isha. He then spent the night at Muzdalifah until the dawn broke. When Fajr arrived, he gave the Iqamah and prayed Subh.\n\nThen he said: \These two prayers are delayed from their usual times"","" and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would not pray them on this day except at this place.\""\n\nThen he stood for the standing (at Muzdalifah).\n\nAbu Bakr (a narrator in the chain) said: \""Ibn Mas'ud (RA) did not narrate the story of his supper between the two prayers as a report from the Prophet (ﷺ)"","" rather it was his own action.\"""""""
